WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.425
Canadian Dental Care Plan.

00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:06.522
Promised as universal dental coverage for Canadians without insurance.

00:00:06.522 --> 00:00:09.795
Rolled out in phases starting 2024.

00:00:09.795 --> 00:00:17.522
Implementation challenges: provider opt-out rates, processing delays, income verification complexity, and eligibility gaps.

00:00:17.522 --> 00:00:20.306
Nine million Canadians were eligible.

00:00:20.306 --> 00:00:22.806
How many actually received care?

00:00:22.806 --> 00:00:26.829
This page tracks the rollout data from official government sources.

00:00:26.829 --> 00:00:28.352
The promise.

00:00:28.352 --> 00:00:34.249
The Canadian Dental Care Plan was announced as part of the NDP-Liberal Supply and Confidence Agreement.

00:00:34.249 --> 00:00:38.068
Nine million Canadians without dental insurance were eligible.

00:00:38.068 --> 00:00:47.136
The plan launched in phases: seniors first in May 2024, then persons with disabilities, children under 18, and finally all eligible adults.

00:00:47.136 --> 00:00:53.761
Income threshold: families under ninety thousand dollars, with co-pays for those between seventy and ninety thousand.

00:00:53.761 --> 00:00:55.670
The delivery pattern.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:59.534
Announce a transformational program during a political agreement.

00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:01.761
Set aggressive timelines.

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:04.749
Contract administration to the private sector.

00:01:04.749 --> 00:01:07.636
Launch before infrastructure is ready.

00:01:07.636 --> 00:01:11.431
Measure success by enrollment numbers rather than care delivered.

00:01:11.431 --> 00:01:16.874
The same pattern as Phoenix Pay, where the system was launched despite warnings that it wasn't ready.

00:01:16.874 --> 00:01:25.659
The dental plan's success will ultimately be measured not by how many cards were issued but by how many Canadians actually received dental care they couldn't access before.
